There it is! The response body is `{"error":"invalid_request","error_description":"HTTPS required"}` — 64 bytes exactly!

The issue is that keycloak requires HTTPS but it's seeing the request as HTTP. When the request comes through nginx from the VPS host, nginx terminates TLS and forwards to keycloak as HTTP. Keycloak then checks `X-Forwarded-Proto` or `X-Forwarded-*` headers to determine the original protocol.

Looking at the nginx config for the `/auth/` location:
```
location /auth/ {
    ...
    proxy_pass http://keycloak_servers/auth/;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $realip_remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Port $server_port;
}
```

It sets `X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme`. The `$scheme` variable in nginx should be `https` when the request comes in on the HTTPS server block (port 443). Let me verify this.

But wait - when I curl from inside the nginx container with `https://localhost`, that should also hit the HTTPS server block with `$scheme = https`. And my curl test worked!

So the issue is specific to curling from the VPS host machine (via `localhost`). When curling from the host, the request goes through Docker networking. Let me check the flow:

1. `curl https://wimsbfp.tech` from VPS host
2. DNS resolves wimsbfp.tech to 127.0.0.1 (or external IP)
3. If going to external IP, it loops back to the VPS
4. Nginx receives the request on port 443
5. Nginx proxies to keycloak

But wait, when I tested from INSIDE the nginx container, I used `localhost` with `Host: wimsbfp.tech`. That went to port 443 (HTTPS).

When testing from the VPS HOST, `curl https://wimsbfp.tech` goes to the external IP (or localhost). If it goes to the external IP, it hits the VPS's public interface, which routes back to nginx.

